/* Chapter 7 board-style questions: single best answer. `answer` is the zero-based index of the correct choice. */
SP.add("ch07", "cases", [
  {"id":"q7-01","tag":"Outcomes","q":"A patient\u2019s depression scale score falls from 30 to 14 after 8 weeks of treatment. This outcome is best described as:","choices":["Recurrence","Response","Remission","Recovery","Relapse"],"answer":1,"why":"A **\u2265 50%** reduction is a **response**; remission requires essentially no symptoms.","pages":"284","whyNot":["Recurrence means depression **returning** after the patient has recovered, not a drop in scores during acute treatment.","","Remission requires essentially all symptoms to clear; a score of 14 still leaves a lot, so this is only a **half-reduction**.","Recovery is remission **sustained** beyond the first several months, which cannot be judged after only 8 weeks.","Relapse is depression **coming back** before full remission or soon after it, the opposite of this improvement."],"whyNotPages":["285","","285","285","285"]},
  {"id":"q7-02","tag":"Outcomes","q":"Depression returns 3 years after a patient fully recovered. This is:","choices":["Relapse","Partial response","Recurrence","Nonresponse","Treatment resistance by definition"],"answer":2,"why":"Return **after recovery** is a **recurrence**; return before full remission or within months of it is a relapse.","pages":"285","whyNot":["Relapse is a return before full remission or within the first months after it; three years after recovery is **too late** for that.","Partial response describes some **improvement** during treatment, not depression returning after a full recovery.","","Nonresponse means no meaningful improvement **during** treatment, whereas this patient had already fully recovered.","Treatment resistance describes illness that **persists** through successive treatments, not a return after full recovery."],"whyNotPages":["285","284","","284","286"]},
  {"id":"q7-03","tag":"Outcomes","q":"According to the book, the continuation phase of treatment typically lasts:","choices":["2\u20134 weeks","Lifelong","6\u201312 weeks","1 or more years","4\u20139 months"],"answer":4,"why":"Acute **6\u201312 weeks**, continuation **4\u20139 months**, maintenance **1+ years** (Figure 7-3).","pages":"285","whyNot":["**2\u20134 weeks** is shorter than any phase in the book; acute treatment alone is listed as 6\u201312 weeks.","**Lifelong** is not one of the phases shown; the last phase, maintenance, is described as lasting one or more years.","**6\u201312 weeks** is the acute treatment phase, which comes before continuation rather than being it.","**One or more years** is the maintenance phase that follows continuation, not the continuation phase itself.",""],"whyNotPages":["285","285","285","285",""]},
  {"id":"q7-04","tag":"Effectiveness","q":"After four sequential 12-week monotherapies, approximately what proportion of unipolar patients achieve remission?","choices":["One-quarter","Nine-tenths","One-half","Two-thirds","One-third"],"answer":3,"why":"About **one-third** remit on the first treatment and about **two-thirds** after four (Figure 7-4).","pages":"286","whyNot":["**One-quarter** understates it; about one-third already remit on the first treatment, so the total after four must be higher.","**Nine-tenths** overstates it; each added trial yields fewer remitters, so the cumulative figure stalls well short of this.","**One-half** is too low; the cumulative remission after four sequential monotherapies reaches roughly two-thirds.","","**One-third** is the remission rate after the first treatment only, not the cumulative figure after four trials."],"whyNotPages":["286","286","286","","286"]},
  {"id":"q7-05","tag":"Effectiveness","q":"Which is among the LEAST common residual symptoms after treatment of a major depressive episode?","choices":["Suicidal ideation","Painful physical symptoms","Fatigue","Insomnia","Problems concentrating"],"answer":0,"why":"Depressed mood, **suicidal ideation** and psychomotor retardation are the least common residuals (Figure 7-5).","pages":"286","whyNot":["","Painful physical complaints are among the **most common** persisting symptoms in nonremitters, so they are not the least common.","**Fatigue** is listed with insomnia and pain among the most common residual symptoms, not the least common.","**Insomnia** is one of the leading residual symptoms after treatment, so it is not among the rarest.","**Problems concentrating** is on the list of common residual symptoms, along with lack of interest, not the least common."],"whyNotPages":["","286","286","286","286"]},
  {"id":"q7-06","tag":"Effectiveness","q":"For patients who remit only after four treatment steps, the relapse rate is approximately:","choices":["10%","70%","33%","90%","50%"],"answer":1,"why":"About **70%** at 6 months, the same as non-remitters: the protection of remission disappears.","pages":"287","whyNot":["**10%** greatly understates it; relapse after remission climbs with each added treatment step toward about 70%.","","**33%** is the relapse rate for remitters after the **first** treatment (at 12 months), not after four steps.","**90%** overstates it; the highest figure shown for relapse after four treatments is about 70%.","**50%** is an intermediate value; relapse keeps rising with additional steps, reaching about 70% by the fourth."],"whyNotPages":["287","","287","287","287"]},
  {"id":"q7-07","tag":"Mood stabilizers","q":"A drug that prevents recurrence of bipolar depression is described in the book as:","choices":["Stabilizing from below","Treating from above","A true mood stabilizer by FDA definition","Stabilizing from above","Treating from below"],"answer":0,"why":"Preventing **depressive** relapse/recurrence = **stabilize from below** (Figure 7-8).","pages":"288","whyNot":["","**Treating from above** reduces current manic symptoms; it says nothing about preventing depressive episodes.","The FDA position in the book is that **no such thing** as a mood stabilizer exists; the book uses mechanism-based phrases instead.","**Stabilizing from above** means preventing relapse or recurrence of mania, the opposite pole from depression.","**Treating from below** means reducing existing bipolar depression symptoms, not preventing later episodes."],"whyNotPages":["","288","288","288","288"]},
  {"id":"q7-08","tag":"SSRIs","q":"The onset of therapeutic action of SSRIs correlates best with:","choices":["Upregulation of 5HT1A autoreceptors","Immediate SERT blockade at axon terminals","Desensitization of postsynaptic 5HT2C receptors","Increased MAO-A activity","Desensitization of somatodendritic 5HT1A autoreceptors"],"answer":4,"why":"Autoreceptor desensitization disinhibits firing and terminal 5HT release; postsynaptic desensitization tracks **tolerance to side effects**.","pages":"291","whyNot":["Upregulated 5HT1A autoreceptors characterize the **depressed** state before treatment; therapeutic onset follows their downregulation instead.","SERT blockade is immediate, but serotonin does not rise much at axon terminals early, so it cannot explain the **delay**.","Postsynaptic receptor desensitization comes last and tracks tolerance to **side effects**, not therapeutic onset.","**MAO-A** is the enzyme MAOIs inhibit; SSRIs act at the serotonin transporter, not by changing MAO activity.",""],"whyNotPages":["290","291","291","338",""]},
  {"id":"q7-09","tag":"SSRIs","q":"Shortly after starting an SSRI, serotonin rises first:","choices":["In the striatum","In the gut only","At axon terminals in the prefrontal cortex","In the somatodendritic area of the raphe","In the hippocampus"],"answer":3,"why":"5HT rises first near the **cell bodies** in the raphe, stimulating 5HT1A autoreceptors.","pages":"291","whyNot":["The striatum is a projection area; serotonin rises there **later**, after autoreceptors desensitize and firing is disinhibited.","The first rise described is in the **brain's** raphe; gut serotonin matters mainly for side effects via 5HT3 receptors.","Terminal areas such as the prefrontal cortex see the rise only **after** a delay, once neuronal impulse flow turns on.","","The hippocampus is another terminal region, so its serotonin increase is **delayed** compared with the cell-body area."],"whyNotPages":["291","311","292","","291"]},
  {"id":"q7-10","tag":"SSRIs","q":"A patient with hypersomnia, fatigue and psychomotor retardation is started on an SSRI chosen for its 5HT2C antagonism. Which drug?","choices":["Citalopram","Paroxetine","Fluoxetine","Fluvoxamine","Escitalopram"],"answer":2,"why":"**Fluoxetine\u2019s 5HT2C antagonism** is activating, suiting reduced positive affect and hypersomnia.","pages":"293","whyNot":["**Citalopram** is distinguished by its R enantiomer interfering at SERT, not by 5HT2C antagonism or an activating profile.","**Paroxetine** is more calming or sedating early on because of anticholinergic action, the opposite of what this patient needs.","","**Fluvoxamine** is distinguished by \u03c31 receptor binding and anxiolytic properties, not 5HT2C antagonism.","**Escitalopram** is the pure S enantiomer whose actions are almost entirely explained by SERT inhibition, without 5HT2C antagonism."],"whyNotPages":["295","294","","295","296"]},
  {"id":"q7-11","tag":"SSRIs","q":"Which SSRI inhibits nitric oxide synthase, possibly adding to sexual dysfunction?","choices":["Paroxetine","Escitalopram","Fluoxetine","Fluvoxamine","Sertraline"],"answer":0,"why":"**Paroxetine**: M1 anticholinergic, weak NET and **NOS** inhibition (Figure 7-18).","pages":"294","whyNot":["","**Escitalopram** is the SSRI whose effects are almost entirely explained by pure SERT inhibition, with no NOS action named.","**Fluoxetine** adds 5HT2C antagonism and weak NET inhibition, features tied to activation rather than NOS inhibition.","**Fluvoxamine** is distinguished by \u03c31 receptor binding, a different secondary property from NOS inhibition.","**Sertraline** is distinguished by weak DAT inhibition and \u03c31 binding, not nitric oxide synthase inhibition."],"whyNotPages":["","296","293","295","294"]},
  {"id":"q7-12","tag":"SSRIs","q":"Weak dopamine transporter inhibition is a distinguishing property of:","choices":["Escitalopram","Sertraline","Paroxetine","Citalopram","Fluvoxamine"],"answer":1,"why":"**Sertraline** has weak DAT inhibition on top of SERT blockade, plus \u03c31 binding; the DAT action is controversial but may help energy, motivation and concentration.","pages":"294","whyNot":["**Escitalopram** acts almost purely through SERT inhibition, so it has no distinguishing DAT component.","","**Paroxetine** adds muscarinic anticholinergic action, weak NET inhibition and NOS inhibition, not DAT inhibition.","**Citalopram** is distinguished by its R enantiomer, which carries weak antihistamine properties and may interfere with SERT.","**Fluvoxamine** is distinguished by potent \u03c31 binding, not dopamine transporter inhibition."],"whyNotPages":["296","","294","295","295"]},
  {"id":"q7-13","tag":"SSRIs","q":"Why is the lowest dose of escitalopram more predictably effective than the lowest dose of citalopram?","choices":["Escitalopram also blocks NET","Citalopram is an MAO inhibitor","Escitalopram adds 5HT1A partial agonism","The R enantiomer, absent from escitalopram, may interfere with SERT inhibition by the S enantiomer","Escitalopram is a prodrug"],"answer":3,"why":"Removing the **R enantiomer** removes interference at SERT and antihistamine action (Figures 7-20, 7-21).","pages":"296","whyNot":["Escitalopram acts almost purely through **SERT**; adding NET inhibition is what SNRIs do, not this drug.","Citalopram is not an MAO inhibitor; its quirk is a **mixture** of enantiomers, one of which interferes with SERT.","**5HT1A partial agonism** is the added property of vilazodone, not of escitalopram.","","Escitalopram is not a prodrug; it is simply the pure, **active S enantiomer** of citalopram."],"whyNotPages":["296","295","296","","296"]},
  {"id":"q7-14","tag":"SSRIs","q":"Which SSRI binds \u03c31 receptors more potently than sertraline and is used mainly for OCD in the US?","choices":["Fluoxetine","Citalopram","Fluvoxamine","Escitalopram","Paroxetine"],"answer":2,"why":"**Fluvoxamine**: potent \u03c31 binding, possibly agonist; not approved for depression in the US.","pages":"295","whyNot":["**Fluoxetine** is distinguished by 5HT2C antagonism and a long half-life, not by \u03c31 binding.","**Citalopram** is a mixture of R and S enantiomers, with antihistamine properties in the R form, not \u03c31 potency.","","**Escitalopram** is the pure S enantiomer whose pharmacology is almost entirely SERT inhibition.","**Paroxetine** adds anticholinergic, weak NET and NOS actions, and is not the OCD-oriented, \u03c31-potent agent."],"whyNotPages":["293","295","","296","294"]},
  {"id":"q7-15","tag":"SPARIs","q":"Vilazodone differs from SSRIs by adding:","choices":["NET inhibition","MT1/MT2 agonism","5HT2C antagonism","\u03b12 antagonism","5HT1A partial agonism"],"answer":4,"why":"Vilazodone is a **SPARI**: it blocks SERT and also partially stimulates 5HT1A, which speeds and strengthens the autoreceptor desensitization behind antidepressant action.","pages":"296","whyNot":["**NET inhibition** is the added property that defines SNRIs, not SPARIs.","**MT1/MT2 agonism** is the signature of agomelatine, a melatonin agonist.","**5HT2C antagonism** characterizes fluoxetine, mirtazapine and agomelatine rather than vilazodone.","**\u03b12 antagonism** is the main action of mirtazapine, which blocks no transporter.",""],"whyNotPages":["298","306","293","309",""]},
  {"id":"q7-16","tag":"SNRIs","q":"SNRIs increase dopamine in the prefrontal cortex because:","choices":["They release dopamine via VMAT2","They inhibit DAT at clinical doses","Prefrontal dopamine is cleared mainly by NET, since DATs are sparse there","They block D2 autoreceptors","They inhibit COMT"],"answer":2,"why":"Few **DATs** in the PFC; **NET** (with higher affinity for DA than NE) clears DA there (Figure 7-33).","pages":"301","whyNot":["Dopamine release through **VMAT2** inhibition is how amphetamine and MDMA act, not SNRIs.","SNRIs do not inhibit **DAT** except at doses beyond the clinical range, and few DATs exist in the cortex anyway.","","SNRIs act at transporters; **D2 blockade** belongs to the serotonin/dopamine antagonist drugs.","**COMT** does degrade cortical dopamine, but SNRIs are not described as inhibiting it; they block NET."],"whyNotPages":["356","301","","342","301"]},
  {"id":"q7-17","tag":"SNRIs","q":"A patient on venlafaxine is started on a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor. The most likely pharmacological consequence is:","choices":["More venlafaxine relative to desvenlafaxine and less NET inhibition","More desvenlafaxine and more NET inhibition","Conversion to milnacipran","Loss of SERT inhibition","No change in the venlafaxine:desvenlafaxine ratio"],"answer":0,"why":"Blocking **2D6** shifts the ratio toward **parent venlafaxine**, reducing relative NET inhibition.","pages":"302","whyNot":["","Opposite direction: a 2D6 inhibitor **blocks** conversion to desvenlafaxine, so there is less of it and less NET inhibition.","**Milnacipran** is a separate drug, not a metabolite of venlafaxine.","Venlafaxine itself is potent at **SERT** even at low doses, so SERT inhibition is preserved.","The ratio is **variable**, shifting with 2D6 inhibitors, inducers and genetic polymorphisms."],"whyNotPages":["","302","303","302","299"]},
  {"id":"q7-18","tag":"SNRIs","q":"Which SNRI is approved for fibromyalgia but not for depression in the US?","choices":["Duloxetine","Venlafaxine","Levomilnacipran","Milnacipran","Desvenlafaxine"],"answer":3,"why":"**Milnacipran** is approved for fibromyalgia but not unipolar depression in the US; in Europe the situation is reversed, with approval for depression only.","pages":"303","whyNot":["**Duloxetine** treats depression as well as pain, so it is not the one excluded from depression.","**Venlafaxine** is used for depression and several anxiety disorders, not as a fibromyalgia-only drug.","**Levomilnacipran** is the active enantiomer of milnacipran developed for unipolar depression in the US.","","**Desvenlafaxine** is the venlafaxine metabolite developed as its own antidepressant, not a fibromyalgia-only drug."],"whyNotPages":["302","302","303","","302"]},
  {"id":"q7-19","tag":"SNRIs","q":"Which property distinguishes milnacipran from venlafaxine and duloxetine?","choices":["5HT2C antagonism","DAT inhibition","No noradrenergic side effects","Once-weekly dosing","More potent NET than SERT inhibition"],"answer":4,"why":"Milnacipran (and levomilnacipran) are **NET > SERT**; the others are SERT > NET.","pages":"303","whyNot":["**5HT2C antagonism** is a property of fluoxetine, mirtazapine and agomelatine, not milnacipran.","SNRIs, including milnacipran, do not inhibit **DAT** at clinical doses.","Opposite: robust NET inhibition may cause **more** sweating and urinary hesitancy than other SNRIs.","Milnacipran is dosed **twice daily** because of a shorter half-life; once-weekly dosing is a fluoxetine option.",""],"whyNotPages":["293","301","303","303",""]},
  {"id":"q7-20","tag":"SNRIs","q":"The book\u2019s evidence that painful physical symptoms are true symptoms of depression came mainly from studies of:","choices":["Mirtazapine","Duloxetine","Escitalopram","Trazodone","Bupropion"],"answer":1,"why":"**Duloxetine** relieved depression without pain and **pain without depression**.","pages":"302","whyNot":["**Mirtazapine** blocks no transporter; the pain-without-depression evidence came from an SNRI, not this multireceptor drug.","","**Escitalopram** is an SSRI, and SNRIs have shown clear efficacy in pain where SSRIs have not.","**Trazodone** is a SARI known for hypnotic use at low doses, not for pain-and-depression studies.","**Bupropion** is an NDRI aimed at reduced positive affect, not the drug behind the pain findings."],"whyNotPages":["308","","299","311","306"]},
  {"id":"q7-21","tag":"NDRIs","q":"PET studies suggest that therapeutic doses of bupropion occupy what proportion of striatal DATs?","choices":["About 80\u201390%","About 50\u201360%","Nearly 100%","About 10\u201330%","None"],"answer":3,"why":"Only **10\u201315%**, perhaps up to **20\u201330%**: low, slow occupancy that is not abusable.","pages":"304","whyNot":["**80\u201390%** is roughly the SERT occupancy SSRIs need; bupropion's DAT occupancy is far lower.","**50\u201360%** is closer to the DAT occupancy of abusable stimulants or ADHD drugs, much higher than bupropion's.","**Nearly 100%** is not what PET shows; bupropion is only a weak DAT inhibitor.","","PET shows a **small but real** fraction occupied, and bupropion is described as a weak DAT inhibitor."],"whyNotPages":["305","305","304","","304"]},
  {"id":"q7-22","tag":"NDRIs","q":"Which DAT occupancy pattern is characteristic of abusable stimulants such as cocaine?","choices":["Slow, sustained occupancy of 50% or more","No DAT occupancy","Slow, sustained occupancy of 10\u201320%","Occupancy of NET only","Rapid, brief occupancy of 50% or more"],"answer":4,"why":"Abusable stimulants such as cocaine hit **DATs fast, hard and briefly** (50% or more occupied quickly), producing euphoria and reinforcement, unlike slow, low occupancy.","pages":"305","whyNot":["Slow, sustained occupancy of 50% or more, especially with controlled-release forms, is **less abusable** and suits ADHD treatment.","Cocaine acts by blocking **DAT**, so occupancy is certainly not absent.","Slow, low occupancy of 10\u201320% is the pattern suggested for **bupropion**, which is not known to be abusable.","Reinforcing stimulants such as cocaine owe their effect to high-impact **DAT** inhibition, not to NET-only action.",""],"whyNotPages":["306","305","306","294",""]},
  {"id":"q7-23","tag":"NDRIs","q":"Which symptom cluster is bupropion especially targeted at?","choices":["Reduced positive affect","Insomnia","Psychotic symptoms","Mixed manic features","Increased negative affect such as anxiety and guilt"],"answer":0,"why":"The **\u201cdopamine deficiency syndrome\u201d**: loss of joy, interest, energy, alertness and self-confidence.","pages":"306","whyNot":["","Bupropion is generally **activating**; residual insomnia is instead handled with a hypnotic such as low-dose trazodone.","Psychotic depression is where sertraline and fluvoxamine may have an edge via **\u03c31**, not bupropion.","Reuptake inhibitors can cause activation and **mania** in mixed features, which serotonin/dopamine blockers are preferred for.","Anxiety and guilt represent the **opposite** end of the symptom spectrum from reduced positive affect."],"whyNotPages":["","311","294","342","306"]},
  {"id":"q7-24","tag":"Agomelatine","q":"Agomelatine\u2019s mechanism is best described as:","choices":["\u03b12 antagonism plus H1 antagonism","MT1/MT2 agonism plus 5HT2C antagonism","NMDA antagonism","5HT1A agonism plus 5HT3 antagonism","MT1/MT2 antagonism plus SERT inhibition"],"answer":1,"why":"Agomelatine is a **melatonin MT1/MT2 agonist** and a **5HT2C** antagonist, resetting circadian rhythms while disinhibiting dopamine and norepinephrine release in the prefrontal cortex.","pages":"306","whyNot":["**\u03b12 plus H1 antagonism** describes part of mirtazapine, not a melatonin-based drug.","","**NMDA antagonism** is the proposed mechanism of ketamine, not agomelatine.","**5HT1A agonism with 5HT3 antagonism** is part of vortioxetine's profile, alongside SERT inhibition.","Both halves are wrong: agomelatine is an **agonist** at MT1/MT2 and does not inhibit SERT."],"whyNotPages":["308","","329","315","306"]},
  {"id":"q7-25","tag":"Mirtazapine","q":"Mirtazapine increases serotonin release mainly by:","choices":["Stimulating 5HT1A autoreceptors","Inhibiting SERT","Blocking \u03b12 heteroreceptors on serotonin neurons","Blocking 5HT1B/D autoreceptors","Inhibiting MAO-A"],"answer":2,"why":"Mirtazapine blocks no transporter; its **\u03b12 antagonism** removes norepinephrine's brake on serotonin neurons (heteroreceptors) and on its own release, so both 5HT and NE rise.","pages":"309","whyNot":["**5HT1A** stimulation is the added action of vilazodone-type drugs, not how mirtazapine works.","Mirtazapine, unlike nearly every other antidepressant, blocks **no** monoamine transporter.","","Blocking **5HT1B/D** autoreceptors is an action attributed to vortioxetine, not mirtazapine.","**MAO-A** inhibition is the mechanism of MAOIs, which are a different class entirely."],"whyNotPages":["296","308","","318","338"]},
  {"id":"q7-26","tag":"Mirtazapine","q":"\u201cCalifornia rocket fuel\u201d refers to:","choices":["An SNRI plus mirtazapine","An SNRI plus a stimulant","An SSRI plus bupropion","Olanzapine plus fluoxetine","Lithium plus a TCA"],"answer":0,"why":"**California rocket fuel** pairs an SNRI's transporter blockade with mirtazapine's **\u03b12 antagonism**, two synergistic ways to increase serotonin and norepinephrine release.","pages":"309","whyNot":["","An SNRI with a **stimulant** is one of the arousal combinations, aimed at fatigue and low energy.","An SSRI plus **bupropion** is a triple-action combination, not the one named for rocket fuel.","**Olanzapine\u2013fluoxetine** is a combination of an antipsychotic with an SSRI, approved in some countries.","**Lithium** with a tricyclic is an older augmentation approach, unrelated to this name."],"whyNotPages":["","335","333","326","332"]},
  {"id":"q7-27","tag":"Mirtazapine","q":"Mianserin differs from mirtazapine chiefly by additional:","choices":["5HT1A agonism","\u03b11 antagonism","D2 antagonism","MT1 agonism","SERT inhibition"],"answer":1,"why":"Potent **\u03b11** antagonism blunts serotonergic enhancement, so it mainly boosts **NE**.","pages":"309","whyNot":["**5HT1A agonism** is not what separates mianserin from mirtazapine; the only difference is the added \u03b11 effect.","","**D2 antagonism** belongs to antipsychotics, not to the mianserin and mirtazapine profile.","**MT1 agonism** is the signature of agomelatine.","Like mirtazapine, mianserin blocks **no** reuptake pump; SERT inhibition is not its extra feature."],"whyNotPages":["308","","343","306","308"]},
  {"id":"q7-28","tag":"5HT3","q":"Antagonism of brain 5HT3 receptors increases release of acetylcholine and norepinephrine because 5HT3 receptors are located on:","choices":["Astrocytes","Microglia","GABA interneurons, where they are excitatory","NE terminals as autoreceptors","ACh neurons, where they are inhibitory"],"answer":2,"why":"5HT3 stimulation drives **GABA** inhibition; blocking it **disinhibits** downstream neurons.","pages":"311","whyNot":["**Astrocytes** are not where the book places 5HT3 receptors; it locates them on GABA interneurons.","**Microglia** are never named as 5HT3 sites; the relevant brain receptors sit on GABA interneurons.","","Norepinephrine terminals carry **\u03b12** autoreceptors; the 5HT3 effect acts indirectly through GABA.","5HT3 receptors are not on ACh neurons; they are **excitatory** receptors on GABA cells that in turn inhibit ACh neurons."],"whyNotPages":["311","311","","310","313"]},
  {"id":"q7-29","tag":"SARIs","q":"At hypnotic doses (25\u2013150 mg), trazodone acts mainly through antagonism of:","choices":["SERT and 5HT2C","MT1 and MT2","D2 and 5HT1A","5HT2A, \u03b11 and H1 receptors","NET and DAT"],"answer":3,"why":"Low doses engage the highest-affinity targets: **5HT2A, \u03b11 subtypes, H1**; SERT is not saturated.","pages":"315","whyNot":["**SERT** is not saturated at hypnotic doses; it is recruited only at the higher antidepressant range.","**MT1/MT2** are melatonin receptors, the targets of agomelatine, not trazodone.","Trazodone has no **D2** action and stimulates, rather than blocks, 5HT1A receptors.","","**NET and DAT** inhibition describe bupropion-type drugs, not trazodone."],"whyNotPages":["315","306","314","","303"]},
  {"id":"q7-30","tag":"SARIs","q":"Why do SARIs lack the sexual dysfunction, insomnia and anxiety of SSRIs?","choices":["They are 5HT2A agonists","They block 5HT1A receptors","They inhibit MAO","They lack SERT inhibition","They block 5HT2A and 5HT2C receptors"],"answer":4,"why":"Serotonin still reaches **5HT1A**, while **5HT2A/2C** stimulation is blocked (Figure 7-48).","pages":"316","whyNot":["SARIs **block** 5HT2A rather than stimulate it, which is why side effects tied to that receptor are avoided.","Serotonin is still free to act at **5HT1A**, which carries the antidepressant effect, so that receptor is not blocked.","SARIs are not **MAO** inhibitors; MAOIs are a separate drug class.","Trazodone does inhibit **SERT** at antidepressant doses; the sparing of side effects comes from receptor blockade.",""],"whyNotPages":["316","316","338","311",""]},
  {"id":"q7-31","tag":"SARIs","q":"Nefazodone is rarely used today because of:","choices":["Seizures","QTc prolongation","Stevens\u2013Johnson syndrome","Agranulocytosis","Rare liver toxicity"],"answer":4,"why":"**Nefazodone** is a SARI that has fallen out of common use because of rare **liver toxicity**, despite its robust 5HT2A antagonist action.","pages":"311","whyNot":["**Seizures** are linked in the book to peak levels of immediate-release bupropion, not nefazodone.","**QTc prolongation** is tied to higher doses of citalopram.","**Stevens\u2013Johnson syndrome** is the rare serious rash risk of lamotrigine.","**Bone marrow** suppression is a carbamazepine concern in the book, not nefazodone's.",""],"whyNotPages":["306","296","352","350",""]},
  {"id":"q7-32","tag":"Vortioxetine","q":"Vortioxetine\u2019s superiority on the DSST reflects improvement mainly in:","choices":["Language","IQ","Processing speed","Long-term memory","Visual acuity"],"answer":2,"why":"The DSST samples several cognitive domains but most strongly taps **processing speed** (Ringo, the drummer, in the Fab Four), and vortioxetine outperformed other antidepressants on it.","pages":"317","whyNot":["**Language** is not one of the four domains described: attention, executive function, memory and processing speed.","**IQ** is not much improved by drug treatment, and the book contrasts it with the more treatable cognitive symptoms.","","**Memory** is one domain the test touches, but the DSST is described as most prominently measuring processing speed.","**Visual acuity** is a sensory function, not one of the cognitive domains the DSST assesses."],"whyNotPages":["317","316","","317","317"]},
  {"id":"q7-33","tag":"Vortioxetine","q":"Which of vortioxetine\u2019s actions is described as one of its most potent?","choices":["5HT3 antagonism","H1 antagonism","NET inhibition","MAO-B inhibition","D2 antagonism"],"answer":0,"why":"Among vortioxetine's several actions, **5HT3 antagonism** is one of the most potent, disinhibiting downstream release of acetylcholine, dopamine and norepinephrine.","pages":"318","whyNot":["","**H1 antagonism** is a feature of mirtazapine and tricyclics, tied to sedation and weight gain, not vortioxetine's standout action.","Vortioxetine is a **SERT** inhibitor, not a NET inhibitor.","**MAO-B** inhibition is relevant to Parkinson's treatment, not to vortioxetine.","**D2 antagonism** belongs to serotonin/dopamine blockers such as quetiapine, not vortioxetine."],"whyNotPages":["","308","315","338","343"]},
  {"id":"q7-34","tag":"Neurosteroids","q":"Neuroactive steroids are thought to exert antidepressant effects primarily through:","choices":["GABA-B receptors","SERT inhibition","Benzodiazepine-sensitive synaptic GABA-A receptors only","Benzodiazepine-insensitive extrasynaptic GABA-A receptors","NMDA receptors"],"answer":3,"why":"Benzodiazepines are not antidepressant, so **benzodiazepine-insensitive** (tonic) receptors are the likely target.","pages":"322","whyNot":["**GABA-B** is not the target; the book describes neuroactive steroids as acting at GABA-A receptors.","**SERT** inhibition is the SSRI mechanism; neuroactive steroids act on GABA receptors instead.","Benzodiazepines act at these receptors yet lack antidepressant action, so they are **not** the likely target.","","**NMDA** receptors are the proposed target of ketamine, not of neuroactive steroids."],"whyNotPages":["321","289","322","","329"]},
  {"id":"q7-35","tag":"Neurosteroids","q":"Brexanolone for postpartum depression is administered as:","choices":["An oral tablet for 14 days","A 60-hour continuous IV infusion","A monthly depot","An intranasal spray twice weekly","A single IM injection"],"answer":1,"why":"**Brexanolone** is a cyclodextrin-based form of allopregnanolone given as a **60-hour continuous IV infusion**, restoring neuroactive steroid levels that fall sharply after delivery.","pages":"320","whyNot":["Brexanolone is not an oral 14-day course; the **oral** allopregnanolone analogue SAGE-217 is still in testing.","","No **depot** is described; the idea is to restore neuroactive steroid levels quickly with a continuous infusion.","The **intranasal** product in this chapter is esketamine, which is for treatment-resistant depression.","A single injection would not match the long infusion the book says gives patients time to adjust, so it is not **brexanolone**."],"whyNotPages":["322","","321","331","321"]},
  {"id":"q7-36","tag":"Genetics","q":"Which statement about pharmacogenomic testing reflects the book\u2019s view?","choices":["It replaces plasma drug levels","It shifts the probability of response or side effects but cannot guarantee the right drug","It has no role after treatment failure","It is only useful for MAOIs","It identifies the single correct drug for each patient"],"answer":1,"why":"Genetic results **shift the odds** of response, nonresponse or side effects but do not dictate a single drug; the book calls this adding to the weight of the evidence.","pages":"325","whyNot":["Genotyping is **coupled** with plasma drug levels in the book, so it does not replace them.","","Testing is described as most helpful **after** several first-line treatments have failed or not been tolerated.","It concerns **many** drug-metabolizing enzymes and pharmacodynamic genes, not only the MAOIs.","Responses are not all-or-none, so testing cannot **guarantee** one right drug for the individual."],"whyNotPages":["323","","323","323","325"]},
  {"id":"q7-37","tag":"Augmentation","q":"The antidepressant effect of olanzapine\u2013fluoxetine is attributed in part to:","choices":["Combined 5HT2C antagonism from both drugs","NMDA antagonism","\u03b12 agonism","MAO inhibition","D3 partial agonism"],"answer":0,"why":"Both block **5HT2C**; with SERT inhibition it acts as a potent **SERT/5HT2C inhibitor**.","pages":"326","whyNot":["","**NMDA antagonism** is the proposed mechanism of ketamine, not of this antipsychotic\u2013SSRI pair.","Opposite direction: **\u03b12 antagonism**, not agonism, is the action linked to antidepressant release.","**MAO** inhibition belongs to the MAOI class, not to olanzapine or fluoxetine.","**D3** partial agonism is cariprazine's distinctive feature, not olanzapine\u2013fluoxetine's."],"whyNotPages":["","329","309","338","343"]},
  {"id":"q7-38","tag":"Augmentation","q":"Quetiapine\u2019s NET inhibition comes mainly from:","choices":["Its D2 antagonism","CYP2D6 conversion to desvenlafaxine","Its H1 antagonism","The parent drug at low doses","Its active metabolite norquetiapine"],"answer":4,"why":"Quetiapine's NET inhibition is attributed to its active metabolite **norquetiapine**, which, with 5HT2C antagonism, helps explain its antidepressant augmenting action.","pages":"326","whyNot":["**D2 antagonism** accounts for quetiapine's schizophrenia and mania approvals, not its NET inhibition.","Conversion by **2D6** to desvenlafaxine describes venlafaxine, not quetiapine.","**H1 antagonism** is tied to sedation and weight gain, not to NET inhibition.","The parent compound's profile covers other receptors; the book credits its **metabolite** for the NET action.",""],"whyNotPages":["326","302","309","326",""]},
  {"id":"q7-39","tag":"Augmentation","q":"Which serotonin/dopamine agents have the lowest rates of drug-induced parkinsonism, attributed to combined \u03b11 and 5HT2A antagonism?","choices":["Haloperidol and fluphenazine","Aripiprazole and cariprazine only","Brexpiprazole, quetiapine, clozapine and iloperidone","Risperidone and paliperidone","Lurasidone and ziprasidone only"],"answer":2,"why":"Agents with robust **\u03b11 and 5HT2A antagonism** disinhibit nigrostriatal dopamine release into the motor striatum, offsetting D2 blockade and lowering drug-induced parkinsonism.","pages":"328","whyNot":["Conventional D2 blockers lack robust **\u03b11 plus 5HT2A** antagonism, so they are not the lowest-parkinsonism group.","**Aripiprazole** and cariprazine are partial agonists, and aripiprazole is associated with akathisia rather than the lowest parkinsonism.","","**Risperidone** and paliperidone are not among the agents the book names for the lowest parkinsonism.","**Lurasidone** and ziprasidone are not among the agents the book names for the lowest parkinsonism."],"whyNotPages":["328","327","","328","328"]},
  {"id":"q7-40","tag":"Ketamine","q":"Ketamine\u2019s leading hypothesized antidepressant target is:","choices":["SERT","5HT2A receptors","The glycine site of the NMDA receptor","The open-channel PCP site of the NMDA receptor","The benzodiazepine site of GABA-A"],"answer":3,"why":"NMDA antagonism at the **PCP site**; \u03c31, \u03bc-opioid and other actions are debated.","pages":"328\u2013329","whyNot":["**SERT** is a weak ketamine target; the leading hypothesis centers on glutamate receptors.","**5HT2A** agonism is the hallucinogen mechanism of psilocybin, not ketamine's antidepressant target.","The **glycine** site is not named; the book places ketamine's action inside the open channel.","","The **benzodiazepine** site of GABA-A is where neuroactive steroids and benzodiazepines act, not ketamine."],"whyNotPages":["329","358","329","","322"]},
  {"id":"q7-41","tag":"Ketamine","q":"In the mTOR hypothesis, ketamine\u2019s rapid effect requires stimulation of which receptor by the glutamate burst?","choices":["mGluR2","NMDA","Kainate","AMPA","GABA-B"],"answer":3,"why":"Glutamate acts at **AMPA** receptors while NMDA receptors are blocked, triggering ERK/AKT \u2192 mTOR.","pages":"330","whyNot":["**mGluR2** is not part of the mTOR hypothesis; the glutamate burst acts on AMPA receptors.","**NMDA** receptors are blocked by ketamine, so they are not the receptors stimulated by the burst.","**Kainate** receptors are not named in the hypothesis; AMPA receptors are.","","**GABA-B** receptors are not part of the mTOR hypothesis described."],"whyNotPages":["330","330","330","","330"]},
  {"id":"q7-42","tag":"Ketamine","q":"Esketamine is:","choices":["A ketamine prodrug taken orally","The R enantiomer given IV","The S enantiomer of ketamine given intranasally","The active metabolite of dextromethorphan","A deuterated ketamine"],"answer":2,"why":"**S-ketamine**, an intranasal augmenting agent for treatment-resistant depression.","pages":"331","whyNot":["Esketamine is given **intranasally** and is itself active, not an oral prodrug.","Esketamine is the **S**, not the R, enantiomer, and it is given intranasally, whereas intravenous ketamine is racemic.","","**Dextromethorphan** is a separate NMDA antagonist under study, not the source of esketamine.","**Deuteration** was applied to dextromethorphan in the book, not to ketamine."],"whyNotPages":["331","328","","354","354"]},
  {"id":"q7-43","tag":"Combos","q":"Which combination provides a double boost of norepinephrine with single boosts of serotonin and dopamine?","choices":["SNRI plus NDRI","SSRI plus buspirone","SSRI plus NDRI","SSRI plus mirtazapine","SNRI plus modafinil"],"answer":0,"why":"Both drugs block **NET**; the SNRI adds 5HT and bupropion adds DA (Figure 7-64B).","pages":"334","whyNot":["","**SSRI plus buspirone** adds 5HT1A partial agonism, an approach similar to vilazodone, not extra norepinephrine.","An **SSRI** plus an NDRI gives a single boost to all three monoamines, not a double norepinephrine boost.","**SSRI plus mirtazapine** is not the combination shown; the figures pair mirtazapine with an SNRI.","An SNRI with **modafinil** gives single boosts to serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine."],"whyNotPages":["","333","334","334","335"]},
  {"id":"q7-44","tag":"TCAs","q":"Which pharmacological action of TCAs is responsible for lethality in overdose?","choices":["H1 antagonism","Voltage-sensitive sodium channel blockade","5HT2A antagonism","SERT inhibition","\u03b11 antagonism"],"answer":1,"why":"Sodium channel blockade causes **seizures, coma, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest**.","pages":"336\u2013337","whyNot":["**H1** antagonism causes sedation and weight gain, not death in overdose.","","**5HT2A** antagonism may contribute to the therapeutic profile of some tricyclics, not overdose toxicity.","**SERT** inhibition is a therapeutic action of some tricyclics, not the cause of overdose lethality.","**\u03b11** antagonism causes orthostatic hypotension and dizziness, not the lethal overdose syndrome."],"whyNotPages":["336","","336","335","336"]},
  {"id":"q7-45","tag":"TCAs","q":"Which tricyclic is the most potent serotonin reuptake inhibitor and has anti-OCD efficacy?","choices":["Desipramine","Nortriptyline","Maprotiline","Protriptyline","Clomipramine"],"answer":4,"why":"**Clomipramine** has equal or greater SERT inhibition than NET inhibition and anti-OCD efficacy; the other listed tricyclics are more NET-selective.","pages":"335","whyNot":["**Desipramine** is one of the tricyclics more selective for NET inhibition.","**Nortriptyline** is among the tricyclics that favor NET inhibition over SERT.","**Maprotiline** is listed with the NET-selective group, not as a potent serotonin reuptake inhibitor.","**Protriptyline** is also named among the NET-selective tricyclics.",""],"whyNotPages":["335","336","335","336",""]},
  {"id":"q7-46","tag":"MAOIs","q":"Selective MAO-B inhibition at selective doses is NOT antidepressant because:","choices":["MAO-B is absent from the brain","MAO-B metabolizes 5HT and NE only at high concentrations, and MAO-A still clears DA","MAO-B only metabolizes tyramine","MAO-B inhibitors block SERT","MAO-B inhibitors are reversible"],"answer":1,"why":"**MAO-B** handles serotonin and norepinephrine only at high concentrations, and **MAO-A** keeps clearing dopamine, so selective MAO-B blockade adds little antidepressant effect.","pages":"338","whyNot":["**MAO-B** is present in the brain, along with MAO-A, so absence is not the reason.","","MAO-B also handles dopamine and trace amines; tyramine is a substrate of **both** forms.","MAOIs inhibit an **enzyme**, not the serotonin transporter, so this misdescribes them.","Reversibility is not the reason; what matters is that **MAO-A** still clears serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine."],"whyNotPages":["338","","337","337","338"]},
  {"id":"q7-47","tag":"MAOIs","q":"Which MAOI is metabolized to l-amphetamine and l-methamphetamine?","choices":["Iproniazid","Tranylcypromine","Phenelzine","Selegiline","Isocarboxazid"],"answer":3,"why":"**Selegiline** turns into l-amphetamine and l-methamphetamine in the body, adding amphetamine-like dopamine release; tranylcypromine instead has an amphetamine-like structure itself.","pages":"337","whyNot":["**Iproniazid** was the anti-tuberculosis drug that first revealed MAOI antidepressant action; no amphetamine metabolites are described for it.","**Tranylcypromine** is itself modeled on amphetamine's structure, rather than being metabolized to those compounds.","**Phenelzine** is listed only as an irreversible MAOI, with no amphetamine metabolites described.","","**Isocarboxazid** is likewise listed only as an irreversible MAOI, without amphetamine-like metabolites."],"whyNotPages":["336","337","337","","337"]},
  {"id":"q7-48","tag":"MAOIs","q":"According to the book, which MAOI interaction problem is probably more clinically important than dietary tyramine?","choices":["Smoking","Caffeine","Alcohol","Grapefruit juice","Drug\u2013drug interactions"],"answer":4,"why":"Drug interactions (**sympathomimetics**, **serotonin reuptake inhibitors**) are more common and can be lethal.","pages":"338","whyNot":["**Smoking** is not an interaction named in the book; the two dangerous kinds involve sympathomimetic or serotonergic drugs.","**Caffeine** is not described as an MAOI problem; the book highlights sympathomimetic and serotonin reuptake inhibitor interactions.","**Alcohol** is not the interaction the book emphasizes; drug combinations that raise blood pressure or cause serotonin syndrome matter more.","**Grapefruit juice** is not named; the dietary issue discussed is tyramine, which the book rates below drug interactions.",""],"whyNotPages":["338","338","338","338",""]},
  {"id":"q7-49","tag":"Bipolar","q":"According to Table 7-1, which agent is approved for bipolar depression but not for acute mania?","choices":["Quetiapine","Ziprasidone","Lurasidone","Cariprazine","Aripiprazole"],"answer":2,"why":"**Lurasidone** was never tested for mania; quetiapine and cariprazine are approved for both.","pages":"343","whyNot":["**Quetiapine** is approved for bipolar depression and for mania, so it is not the exception.","**Ziprasidone** is listed for mania and maintenance, not for bipolar depression.","","**Cariprazine** is approved for both acute mania and bipolar depression.","**Aripiprazole** is approved for mania but, per the book, not for bipolar depression, the reverse of this pattern."],"whyNotPages":["343","326","","345","327"]},
  {"id":"q7-50","tag":"Bipolar","q":"Which serotonin/dopamine agent is the only one with a large randomized trial showing efficacy in unipolar depression with mixed features?","choices":["Lurasidone","Asenapine","Olanzapine","Aripiprazole","Quetiapine"],"answer":0,"why":"**Lurasidone** has the sole large multicenter randomized study of unipolar depression with mixed features among these drugs, and it worked well without triggering mania.","pages":"343","whyNot":["","**Asenapine** has evidence for mania with mixed features, but no large unipolar mixed-features trial is described.","**Olanzapine** has post hoc support for mania with mixed features of depression; depression with mixed features of mania is unstudied for it.","**Aripiprazole** is not approved for bipolar depression and has no such trial described.","**Quetiapine** has only post hoc support in mania with mixed depressive features; the reverse condition remains unstudied."],"whyNotPages":["","326","343","327","343"]},
  {"id":"q7-51","tag":"D3","q":"Cariprazine\u2019s antidepressant action is linked to D3 partial agonism/antagonism at:","choices":["D3 receptors in the substantia nigra only","Spinal D3 receptors","Postsynaptic D3 receptors in the prefrontal cortex","Presynaptic D3 autoreceptors in the ventral tegmental area","D3 receptors in the pituitary"],"answer":3,"why":"Blocking **VTA** D3 autoreceptors disinhibits mesocortical DA onto **D1** receptors; the PFC has few D3 receptors.","pages":"344\u2013345","whyNot":["The **substantia nigra** is the nigrostriatal motor circuit tied to \u03b11 antagonism; D3 action for mood is in the VTA.","The **spinal** cord is not part of the mesocortical explanation; the relevant D3 autoreceptors sit in the VTA.","The prefrontal cortex has **few** D3 receptors, so postsynaptic cortical D3 blockade cannot explain it.","","The **pituitary** is not part of the explanation; the key D3 autoreceptors are on VTA dopamine cell bodies."],"whyNotPages":["328","344","345","","344"]},
  {"id":"q7-52","tag":"Bipolar","q":"In mania, lithium or valproate are commonly added to serotonin/dopamine blockers. In schizophrenia, this is:","choices":["Done only with clozapine","Contraindicated because of toxicity","Standard first-line practice","Required for maintenance","Not done, because they do not clearly augment efficacy"],"answer":4,"why":"Lithium and valproate are added to serotonin/dopamine blockers in mania to boost response and prevent relapse, but in **schizophrenia** they do not clearly add efficacy, so it is not done.","pages":"342","whyNot":["The lack of benefit is not limited to **clozapine**; it applies to serotonin/dopamine blockers in schizophrenia generally.","The reason given is no clear added **efficacy**, not toxicity.","It is standard in **mania**, not schizophrenia, where the add-on is not clearly useful.","In mania they are added to prevent relapse, but this is not a **requirement** for schizophrenia maintenance.",""],"whyNotPages":["342","342","342","342",""]},
  {"id":"q7-53","tag":"Lithium","q":"Which enzyme in the phosphatidylinositol system is a candidate target of lithium?","choices":["Inositol monophosphatase","Adenylate cyclase","Monoamine oxidase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","COMT"],"answer":0,"why":"Lithium inhibits **inositol monophosphatase**; GSK-3, PKC and G proteins are other candidates.","pages":"345","whyNot":["","**Adenylate cyclase** is not among the candidates; the book names inositol monophosphatase, G proteins, GSK-3 and protein kinase C.","**MAO** is the target of the MAOI antidepressants, not of lithium.","**Tyrosine hydroxylase** is not named; lithium's candidates are signal transduction sites.","**COMT** degrades dopamine that diffuses away in the prefrontal cortex; it is not a lithium target."],"whyNotPages":["","346","337","346","301"]},
  {"id":"q7-54","tag":"Lithium","q":"Which long-term organ effects of lithium are mentioned?","choices":["Bone marrow and skin","Liver and pancreas","Thyroid and kidney","Retina and cochlea","Heart valves and lungs"],"answer":2,"why":"Potential long-term effects on **thyroid and kidney**; narrow therapeutic window.","pages":"346","whyNot":["**Bone marrow** toxicity is a carbamazepine concern and rash a lamotrigine one, not lithium's long-term issues.","**Liver and pancreas** warnings are listed for valproate, not lithium.","","**Retina and cochlea** are not mentioned; lithium's listed effects are GI, weight, tremor, plus thyroid and kidney.","**Heart and lungs** are not named for lithium; cardiac concerns in the chapter relate to tricyclic overdose."],"whyNotPages":["350","350","","346","337"]},
  {"id":"q7-55","tag":"Anticonvulsants","q":"According to Table 7-3, lamotrigine is strongest at:","choices":["Treating from above only","Stabilizing from above and treating/stabilizing from below","None of the bipolar phases","Treating acute mania","Epilepsy only"],"answer":1,"why":"Lamotrigine: stabilize from above ++++, treat from below +++, stabilize from below ++++; treat from above only +/\u2212.","pages":"347","whyNot":["**Treating from above** is lamotrigine's weakest column, marked only +/\u2212.","","Lamotrigine does work in bipolar disorder, since it is approved for preventing **recurrence** of both poles.","Lamotrigine is **not** approved for mania, and the slow titration makes acute antimanic benefit hard to show.","Lamotrigine is an anticonvulsant but also has strong **bipolar** actions per the table."],"whyNotPages":["347","","350","350","347"]},
  {"id":"q7-56","tag":"Anticonvulsants","q":"Which anticonvulsant causes notable CYP3A4 induction and early bone marrow suppression?","choices":["Lamotrigine","Carbamazepine","Oxcarbazepine","Topiramate","Valproate"],"answer":1,"why":"**Carbamazepine**; oxcarbazepine has fewer 3A4 interactions and less marrow toxicity.","pages":"350","whyNot":["**Lamotrigine** is generally well tolerated apart from rash, not marked by 3A4 induction or marrow suppression.","","**Oxcarbazepine** has less marrow toxicity and fewer 3A4 interactions than the answer drug.","**Topiramate** is linked to weight loss and sedation, not 3A4 induction or marrow suppression.","**Valproate** needs blood count monitoring but is not the one with notable **3A4** induction."],"whyNotPages":["350","","352","352","350"]},
  {"id":"q7-57","tag":"Anticonvulsants","q":"Valproate\u2019s possible actions on signal transduction include all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Inhibition of MARCKS","Inhibition of PKC","Activation of GSK-3","Activation of BCL2","Activation of ERK"],"answer":2,"why":"Valproate, like lithium, may **inhibit** GSK-3 (along with PKC and MARCKS), so describing GSK-3 as activated is the false item; ERK and BCL2 are the activated signals.","pages":"349","whyNot":["Inhibition of **MARCKS** is one of valproate's described signaling actions, so it is not the exception.","Inhibition of **PKC** is also described for valproate, so it is not the exception.","","Activation of **BCL2** is listed among valproate's neuroprotective signals, so it is not the exception.","Activation of **ERK** is listed among valproate's plasticity-promoting signals, so it is not the exception."],"whyNotPages":["349","349","","349","349"]},
  {"id":"q7-58","tag":"Anticonvulsants","q":"Oxcarbazepine exerts its action mainly through:","choices":["\u03b12\u03b4 binding","Direct GABA-T inhibition","Its epoxide metabolite","Conversion to licarbazepine, whose S enantiomer is eslicarbazepine","Conversion to carbamazepine"],"answer":3,"why":"A prodrug for the monohydroxy derivative **licarbazepine**; S form = **eslicarbazepine**.","pages":"352","whyNot":["**\u03b12\u03b4** binding is the target of gabapentin and pregabalin, not oxcarbazepine.","Direct **GABA-T** inhibition is a proposed valproate-type action, not oxcarbazepine's main one.","The book describes a **monohydroxy** derivative, not an epoxide metabolite, as the active form.","","Oxcarbazepine is structurally related to carbamazepine but becomes **licarbazepine**, not carbamazepine."],"whyNotPages":["352","348","352","","352"]},
  {"id":"q7-59","tag":"Future","q":"Bupropion is paired with dextromethorphan partly because bupropion:","choices":["Inhibits CYP2D6, raising dextromethorphan levels","Reduces seizure risk","Blocks \u03c31 receptors","Is an NMDA agonist","Induces CYP3A4"],"answer":0,"why":"Dextromethorphan is rapidly metabolized by **2D6**; bupropion inhibits 2D6 and adds NDRI action.","pages":"353\u2013354","whyNot":["","**Seizure** risk with bupropion relates to peak levels of immediate-release forms, not to this pairing.","**\u03c31** binding is a dextromethorphan property, not bupropion's reason for being paired.","Dextromethorphan is the **NMDA antagonist**; bupropion is not an NMDA agonist.","Bupropion **inhibits** 2D6; CYP3A4 induction is a carbamazepine property."],"whyNotPages":["","306","354","353","350"]},
  {"id":"q7-60","tag":"Future","q":"Psilocybin\u2019s hallucinogenic actions are most closely linked to:","choices":["D2 agonism","5HT3 antagonism","\u03ba-opioid agonism","NMDA antagonism","5HT2A agonism"],"answer":4,"why":"Psilocybin is predominantly a **5HT2A agonist**; its effects in humans are reversed by 5HT2A antagonists but not by selective D2 antagonists, pointing away from dopamine.","pages":"358","whyNot":["Selective **D2** antagonists do not reverse psilocybin's effects in humans, so dopamine agonism is not the link.","**5HT3** antagonism is an antidepressant mechanism of vortioxetine and mirtazapine, not psilocybin's.","**Kappa-opioid** agonism is not linked to psilocybin; the book ties it to serotonin 5HT2A receptors.","**NMDA** antagonism is ketamine's proposed target, not psilocybin's hallucinogenic mechanism.",""],"whyNotPages":["358","311","358","329",""]}
]);
